WEBVTT

00:00.500 --> 00:03.356
To walk in from Spain, you cross an airport runway.

00:03.756 --> 00:08.098
Since July 2026, this gate has had no routine checks.

00:09.750 --> 00:11.898
Midnight at the Gibraltar frontier.

00:12.000 --> 00:14.833
The Rock sits at the western gate of the Mediterranean.

00:15.233 --> 00:22.106
Historians on both sides count fourteen sieges of it, between 1309 and 1783.

00:22.506 --> 00:26.685
This is why everyone fought over it, told from both sides' records.

00:28.000 --> 00:31.877
In 1782, ten floating batteries came to break it.

00:33.000 --> 00:37.273
In 1941, a runway was laid across the only road to Spain.

00:38.667 --> 00:42.429
So why, today, is the fight over it about paperwork and payrolls?

00:43.833 --> 00:49.801
Austria, England and the Dutch backed a rival for Spain's crown; France backed Madrid's king.

00:50.333 --> 00:53.236
That summer, their fleet sailed into the Bay of Gibraltar.

00:54.625 --> 01:03.112
Its commanders, Admiral George Rooke and Prince George of Hesse-Darmstadt, fought for the rival claimant, "Charles the Third."

01:03.512 --> 01:12.254
This plan, printed in 1705, shows the prize: a walled town that historians on both sides would call the key of the Mediterranean.

01:13.125 --> 01:20.636
Inside, Governor Diego de Salinas had barely a hundred soldiers, by a Spanish count.

01:21.901 --> 01:23.341
He refused to surrender.

01:24.014 --> 01:28.972
The fleet answered with some fifteen thousand rounds, by British and Spanish accounts alike.

01:29.375 --> 01:35.308
On the fourth of August 1704, by the new calendar, Gibraltar surrendered.

01:38.875 --> 01:43.345
British accounts put the defenders at up to five hundred, counting armed townsmen.

01:44.500 --> 01:48.552
Townspeople could stay if they swore loyalty to "Charles the Third."

01:49.632 --> 01:57.979
By a British account, almost all left; by a Spanish one, the largest group settled a league north-east, at San Roque.

01:58.500 --> 02:09.947
In 1713, the Treaty of Utrecht gave it to the British Crown "for ever," but "without any territorial jurisdiction, and without any open communication by land."

02:11.213 --> 02:14.650
If Britain ever gave it up, Spain would have first refusal.

02:15.050 --> 02:20.437
The treaty names the town, castle and port, not the sandy isthmus to the north.

02:20.517 --> 02:23.814
Spain says the isthmus was never ceded.

02:23.988 --> 02:27.204
Britain said in 2015 that Gibraltar includes it.

02:27.625 --> 02:32.618
Within weeks, Spain and France besieged it, into 1705.

02:33.697 --> 02:36.391
Spain tried again in 1727.

02:36.821 --> 02:38.144
Neither siege took it.

02:38.583 --> 02:46.849
So in 1730, Philip the Fifth ordered a stone line across the isthmus, out of gun range, to cut Gibraltar off by land.

02:47.615 --> 02:52.607
The town behind it is still called La Línea: "the line."

02:53.083 --> 02:58.540
Half a century later came a different question: can you starve a rock?

02:59.468 --> 03:05.157
On the twenty-first of June 1779, Madrid cut Gibraltar off from Spain.

03:05.557 --> 03:08.459
Spain had joined France in its war with Britain.

03:09.133 --> 03:15.182
By October, British intelligence put the Spanish army facing the Rock at up to fourteen thousand.

03:15.583 --> 03:19.856
At sea, from mid-July, a Spanish squadron sealed the port.

03:20.529 --> 03:26.683
Based at Algeciras, its boats were there to cut off the food reaching the Rock from Africa.

03:27.583 --> 03:35.374
By January 1780, thistles, dandelions and wild leeks were daily food for many; an egg cost sixpence.

03:35.774 --> 03:38.409
The governor, General Eliott, could hold the walls.

03:38.723 --> 03:40.453
The food had to come by sea.

03:41.708 --> 03:49.301
To break the blockade, Britain sent three relief fleets: 1780, 1781, 1782.

03:49.701 --> 03:55.077
Scurvy spread, until a Danish ship of lemons was brought in, garrison diaries record.

03:55.477 --> 04:04.347
On the twelfth of April 1781, the second relief convoy dropped anchor, and Spain's batteries opened fire on the town.

04:05.276 --> 04:07.296
The townspeople fled south.

04:07.708 --> 04:10.994
Two days later, soldiers broke into the merchants' stores.

04:11.575 --> 04:15.754
A British officer blamed "resentment and intoxication."

04:16.167 --> 04:21.252
By British tallies, the two sides fired over four hundred and sixty thousand rounds.

04:22.167 --> 04:27.392
By September 1782, the garrison counted about seven and a half thousand men.

04:30.167 --> 04:33.499
That summer, they had been digging through the north face.

04:34.172 --> 04:39.223
By early July, Sergeant Ince's thirteen men had cut about eighty-two feet of solid rock.

04:40.625 --> 04:43.284
In July they blasted a hole for air.

04:43.957 --> 04:47.161
A Spanish historian wrote it was big enough for a cannon.

04:48.542 --> 04:55.984
The garrison turned to heat: cannonballs heated red-hot, so that whatever they hit caught fire.

04:57.249 --> 05:02.520
On the eighth of September, they used them in earnest against the Spanish works.

05:02.958 --> 05:11.062
Four days later, the French and Spanish fleets gathered in the bay: forty-seven ships of the line by the British count, fifty by the Spanish.

05:11.642 --> 05:14.696
The Duc de Crillon led the siege.

05:15.096 --> 05:21.133
Their answer to red-hot shot: ten floating batteries, designed by the French engineer d'Arçon.

05:21.806 --> 05:25.637
A French account says he'd asked to test them against red-hot shot first.

05:26.067 --> 05:27.124
It was refused.

05:27.524 --> 05:30.600
The thirteenth of September 1782.

05:31.274 --> 05:35.279
That morning, the batteries moved into position under the garrison's fire.

05:36.208 --> 05:42.930
At ten twenty-five, by the Spanish account, the attackers' guns, some four hundred, opened fire together.

05:44.196 --> 05:48.062
By the next afternoon, all ten batteries were lost.

05:48.462 --> 05:58.133
Over two thousand attackers were lost, by a British estimate that includes prisoners, and by a Spanish one; the British rescued three hundred and fifty-seven.

05:58.533 --> 06:01.006
The blockade never forced a surrender.

06:02.086 --> 06:10.166
In February 1783, word came of a preliminary peace, and on the fifth, the last shot was fired.

06:10.583 --> 06:18.501
Spanish historian Montero put losses near two thousand defenders and, from all causes, six thousand besiegers.

06:19.291 --> 06:24.388
The garrison's records count fewer dead: at least eight hundred and sixty-nine.

06:25.653 --> 06:27.255
Gibraltar stayed British.

06:27.655 --> 06:32.566
Which leaves a question the treaty never answered: where does the Rock end?

06:33.646 --> 06:38.917
Between the walls and the Spanish line lay a strip of sand: the neutral ground.

06:39.333 --> 06:45.904
Around 1810, with Napoleon's armies heading for the Strait, Spain and Britain were allies.

06:46.693 --> 06:49.979
With Spain's consent, the British blew up the Spanish forts.

06:50.379 --> 06:52.201
After that, the edge kept moving.

06:53.130 --> 06:58.192
A Spanish historian describes Britain advancing into the neutral zone step by step.

06:58.592 --> 07:02.423
On the Spanish side, a town grew up behind the old line.

07:03.480 --> 07:06.882
In 1870, it became La Línea de la Concepción.

07:08.292 --> 07:10.289
Remember this street in La Línea.

07:10.789 --> 07:11.973
We'll come back to it.

07:15.292 --> 07:20.319
This was the gap between them: a strip of neutral ground between two towns.

07:21.292 --> 07:27.922
In 1908, Britain told Spain, "as an act of courtesy," that it would fence the edge of the neutral ground.

07:28.711 --> 07:33.947
The fence went up in 1909; Spain does not recognise it as a border.

07:37.292 --> 07:42.586
Behind the fence: a garrison camp, gardens, rifle ranges, a racecourse.

07:42.986 --> 07:46.492
And from 1934, a grass airstrip was built there.

07:47.421 --> 07:50.254
Subscribe if you'd like the next place in this series.

07:51.625 --> 07:54.620
Why build an airfield on disputed ground?

07:55.886 --> 08:02.004
In March 1939, Spain protested that the works stood on "a usurped portion" of its territory.

08:02.404 --> 08:12.284
That November, Spain's foreign minister called the strip a fait accompli, like the Rock itself, while insisting its use breached Spanish territory.

08:13.213 --> 08:20.933
Late in 1939, Britain began a hard runway; in 1941, it was laid across the only road to Spain.

08:21.333 --> 08:27.742
Then Britain pushed it out into the bay, with stone blasted from the north face and rubble from new tunnels.

08:28.167 --> 08:36.619
Inside the Rock, those tunnels held a road about a mile long, a hospital and a power station, dug by British and Canadian tunnellers.

08:38.000 --> 08:44.385
Gibraltar counts about fifty-five kilometres of tunnels from every era, more than its roads above ground.

08:45.500 --> 08:52.327
This 1943 US Army map shows the town house by house, and the runway reaching into the bay.

08:53.708 --> 08:58.677
May 1940: Gibraltar sends its women and children away.

08:59.757 --> 09:03.983
June: Spain turns "non-belligerent" and occupies Tangier.

09:05.063 --> 09:09.800
By 1942, the invasion of North Africa is run from inside the Rock.

09:18.625 --> 09:28.471
For the rest of the war, a Spanish scholar found, Spain protested what it called breaches of Spanish waters; no complaint over the airstrip is on record.

09:29.875 --> 09:39.348
On the night of the seventh of November 1942, by Gibraltar's official record, at least six hundred and fifty aircraft lined the runway.

09:40.022 --> 09:44.433
The invasion fleets, from Britain and the United States, came through the Strait.

09:45.833 --> 09:48.503
After the war, the argument returned to the gate.

09:49.432 --> 09:53.751
In June 1969, Spain closed the land frontier completely.

09:54.424 --> 10:01.146
It followed Gibraltar's new constitution; a Spanish historian says Madrid applied Utrecht strictly.

10:01.583 --> 10:06.146
Britain counted some four thousand six hundred Spanish day workers cut off.

10:07.226 --> 10:09.826
The gate fully reopened in 1985.

10:10.226 --> 10:12.083
The same square, today.

10:12.873 --> 10:19.688
In October 2025, people living in Spain held more than four in ten of Gibraltar's jobs.

10:20.088 --> 10:24.198
The UK government puts the daily crossers higher, at about fifteen thousand.

10:24.697 --> 10:26.926
People on foot still cross the runway.

10:27.583 --> 10:29.092
And on the other side of the gate.

10:31.583 --> 10:32.791
Remember this street?

10:33.464 --> 10:44.041
In 2024, La Línea's unemployment was twenty-eight point six percent: the highest of a hundred and thirteen Spanish cities.

10:44.441 --> 10:45.439
Here's the twist.

10:46.368 --> 10:51.035
Gibraltar's government puts output per resident at about eighty-five thousand pounds.

10:51.435 --> 10:59.295
That spreads output over the thirty-eight thousand who live there, and leaves out the people in Spain who hold fourteen thousand of its jobs.

11:00.223 --> 11:04.891
Our read: part of the work behind that number is done from the other side.

11:06.083 --> 11:13.850
In July 2026, Britain and the EU signed a treaty; the next day, routine checks here ended.

11:14.250 --> 11:17.442
It says it changes neither side's position on sovereignty.

11:18.232 --> 11:28.170
In July, Gibraltar's government said the old fence was coming down, with a new one a few metres behind; only about a hundred and fifty metres would stay open.

11:29.542 --> 11:39.690
As of 18 October 2026, the treaty is in force only provisionally, pending a European Parliament vote indicated for the fourteenth of December.

11:40.090 --> 11:42.378
So why did everyone fight over this place?

11:43.167 --> 11:45.489
Where it sits, and the sea that could feed it.

11:46.069 --> 11:48.043
A wall in 1704.

11:48.473 --> 11:50.261
A line in 1730.

11:50.934 --> 11:52.780
A fence in 1909.

11:53.453 --> 11:56.565
A runway in 1941.

12:05.000 --> 12:09.087
In 1730, Spain ordered a line to cut the Rock off by land.

12:09.760 --> 12:14.125
Today, four in ten of the Rock's jobs are held by people who live beyond it.

12:14.799 --> 12:17.840
The last siege ended in 1783.

12:18.340 --> 12:19.605
The argument didn't.

12:20.534 --> 12:24.365
Next in Contested Places: London, 1666.
